whenever people ask me what kind of  games I like to play role-playing games  is generally my go-to response and  Bethesda's Elder Scrolls series is a  huge part of the reason why I've been  playing and replaying that series ever  since I first played morrowind as a kid  I love these games and that's a part of  the reason I've been so skeptical about  the Elder Scrolls online the next  installment into the epic saga has some  Elder Scrolls fans like me worried that  the move to the MMO format will make the  game lose out on what it's done so  successfully in the past if this new  game is gonna be any good it's going to  have to do a few things to prove itself  to its hardcore fans it may occasionally  be difficult to remember all the details  of all the quest lines you're involved  in at any one time in Skyrim but at the  end of the day there's a spectacular  heroic narrative there if you've played  your cards right chances are you've  ended up as the one and only dragon born  Archmage of winterhold  guild master of the Thieves Guild now  listen up the harbinger and then some  when you put them all together like that  it sounds really dumb but the one true  hero narrative that is being the best at  everything in Tamriel is a huge part of  the Elder Scrolls series very in life  the main complication with the Elder  Scrolls online now that the format is  massively multiplayer you're all of a  sudden faced with all these other people  who are all going to be the best in  Tamriel  and that soul one true hero crown is  threatened  obviously this already happened with  anybody who played Skyrim but with so  many other players around you're awfully  confronted with the reality that you  aren't all that special since an MMO  doesn't offer that appeal of being the  singular hero Elder Scrolls online needs  to offer the player something different  but still keep the classic heroic  narrative intact  while a memos have their own set of  funds on redefining hooks but those that  have additional hurdles to overcome when  it comes to turning the elderscrolls  into this kind of game Skyrim fans are  likely to come into the Elder Scrolls  online with certain expectations which  are difficult to emulate in an MMO for  example the graphics are a far cry from  even an unmodern Skyrim game the  animations leave loads to be desired and  the combat isn't going to be as tight  but the quality of questing will be one  of the biggest deciding factors for the  favour of gamers now not all MMO suffer  from this but there can certainly be an  overwhelming number of relatively  insignificant quests from relatively  insignificant NPCs for example gather  ten assorted carcasses from a certain  cave and return for a random reward the  Elder Scrolls online needs to prove that  it can still retain the memorable  characters and intriguing quest lines  from its parent franchise so far they  seem to be doing this well in cutscenes  but for Skyrim fans to stay interested  that needs to transfer quite clearly to  the core of the game alright so I think  it's about the time in this video where  I need to come out and say what we're  all thinking  basically the Elder Scrolls online needs  to not be [ __ ] Skyrim populated by jerks  who so often frequent multiplayer games  the Elder Scrolls online already needs  to be monumentally scaled down in  performance and graphics to function as  an MMO which is why competing with a  single-player RPG on that front is not  going to be an option it's never going  to be as pretty or immersive as Skyrim  the one thing it can do though is make  multiplayer mean something if  multiplayer is the crowning glory of  this title then it needs to be  intrinsically linked to the way players  approach their game the flaws in the one  true hero narrative could be mended by  the strong suggestion that you're  fighting for your faction and all your  guild the sense that you're a part of  something larger and not just one of 30  dudes exploring the same dungeon trying  to be the one true savor of Tamriel  really could be the thing that draws in  skyrim fans where we've had an alternate  take on last week's episode about Dark  Souls saying why the game is just so  amazing just try Dark Souls 1 and I  loved it until I had to replay the same  stretch a million times and just gave up  I admire  game and the difficulty is kind of  refreshing but I want to jump into a  game when I play not replay the same  pots over and over just a personal  preference definitely a great game  that's fair enough  Dark Souls can be easier to admire than  it is to love especially when you've  died a millimeter away from the next  bonfire for the hundredth time but as  these net points out in response Dark  Souls to try to remedy some of these  problems by reducing the number of  respawning enemies either way though I  think I'm inclined to agree with Tommy  c77 who writes last night I met a random  guy on Dark Souls 2 and we spent a few  hours helping each other out with bosses  talk about twenty attempts but we  finally beat the ancient dragon together  after figuring out all the bosses moves  and how to work effectively as a team  speaking as someone who got 100%  achievements on Dark Souls 1 I can  honestly say the struggle and torment is  worth it back to this week's topic let  me know whether you think Elder Scrolls  online has done enough to impress Skyrim  fans or whether you reckon the game will  find an audience outside of the original  franchise leave me all of your thoughts  and feelings in that comment section and  I'll see you next week
 

